<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paolo Guinigi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1372">1372</a>–<a href="/years/1432">1432</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paolo Guinigi was lord of Lucca from 1400 to 1430, from the city's leading merchant family. He ruled as signore behind the outward forms of the republic and commissioned the tomb of his wife Ilaria del Carretto from Jacopo della Quercia. He was overthrown in 1430 during a Florentine attack and died a prisoner in Pavia in 1432.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paolo-guinigi.jpg" alt="Paolo Guinigi" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonimo_toscano,_ritratto_ideale_di_paolo_guinigi,_1590_ca..JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paolo was born in Lucca, the youngest son of Francesco Guinigi, of one of the leading families of the city. In 1389 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and then, from 1390 to 1392, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> to look after the family's business. In 1392 he entered Lucca's General Council and later held the office of Anziano, or Elder.</p><p>The family was torn by violence and disease. His brother Antonio Guinigi killed the head of the family, their elder brother Lazzaro, and Paolo fought against him. Another brother, Bartolomeo, died with others in a plague that struck Lucca, and Paolo also fell ill. On 21 November 1400 he received the titles of Capitano e Difensore del Popolo, which made him the effective lord of the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the same year he escaped a plot hatched by a distant relative, Nicolao Guinigi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Lucca. His first wife, Maria Caterina Antelminelli, linked him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castruccio-castracani">Castruccio Castracani</a>, the condottiero who had been lord of Lucca early in the 14th century, but she died childless. His second wife, Ilaria del Carretto of a noble Ligurian family, bore him a son, Ladislao, and died in 1406.</p><p>Paolo strengthened the walls of Lucca and in 1401 began to rebuild the Augustan Fortress, built by Castracani in 1322 and dismantled in 1370. He encouraged the trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a> marble, banking and the making of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, calling in masters from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and kept peace or alliance with the other Italian states, among them the Papal States.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lucca's alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> drew a Florentine attack in 1429. Florentine troops ravaged the countryside and besieged the city, and his son Ladislao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a> came to its aid. A popular revolt followed, which ended his rule in 1430 and restored the republic. He died in Pavia in 1432.</p><p>His patronage outlived him. Ilaria's funerary monument by Jacopo della Quercia stands in the Cathedral of Lucca, and the same sculptor's polyptych of the Madonna and Child with Saints Lawrence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> and Frediano is in the Basilica of San Frediano. The Villa Guinigi inside the walls is now the Guinigi Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paolo Guinigi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Paolo Guinigi</b> ruled as lord from 1400 to 1430.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Republic of Lucca</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lucca, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Pavia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.85,10.52&amp;year=1411" aria-label="Lucca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Paolo Guinigi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5645 3797h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5645 3797h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.85,10.52&amp;year=1411">What was here in 1411? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
